The assert statement is used when debugging code. The assert keyword lets you test if a condition in your code returns True, if not, the program will raise an AssertionError. We often use it to make sure that a particular statement is true before our program runs. 

The above code checks in the `lambdavec` is approximately equal to `lambda` times `vec`, where `lambda` is one of the eigenvalues from the matrix `mtx`, and `vec` is one of the corresponding eigenvectors. 

The `np.allclose` function is used to determine if two sets of numbers are almost equal. If they are approximately equal, the assert statement will not raise an error.--------------------
import numpy as np

mtx = np.random.random((10,10))

evalues, evectors = np.linalg.eig(mtx)

lambda_ = evalues[0]
vec = evectors[:, 0]

lambdavec = mtx @ vec

# lambdavec is close to lambda times vec

assert np.allclose(lambdavec, lambda_ * vec)

